      <h1>How Surety Bonds Work</h1>
      <p>A surety bond is a three-party agreement: the principal promises the obligee, and the surety guarantees that promise.</p>
      <AuthorBio />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The Three Parties</h2>
      <p>Every surety bond has three parties: the principal who needs the bond, the obligee who requires it, and the surety that guarantees the principal’s promise.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://surety.org/surety-fidelity/what-is-surety/">Surety &amp; Fidelity Association of America</a> describes a surety bond as a written agreement in which the surety guarantees the obligee that the principal will perform the bond, the statute, or the contract.</p>
      <p><strong>The Principal</strong> is the person or business required to get the bond. The principal is promising to follow the license rules, finish the project, or meet the court order.</p>
      <p><strong>The Obligee</strong> is the party requiring the bond, usually a government agency, a court, or a project owner.</p>
      <p><strong>The Surety</strong> is the company that signs the bond. If the principal fails the obligation, the surety pays the obligee, then seeks reimbursement from the principal.</p>

      <h2>How a bond differs from insurance</h2>
      <p>Insurance pays the policyholder for a covered loss. A surety bond pays the obligee, and the principal reimburses the surety.</p>
      <p>That reimbursement is why underwriting looks at the principal. The <a href="/guides/bond-vs-insurance/">bonds versus insurance guide</a> walks through who gets paid.</p>

      <h2>When a bond is required</h2>
      <p>A bond is required when a statute, a license, or a contract says the work cannot start without it.</p>
      <p>On federal construction, <a href="https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title40-section3131&num=0&edition=prelim">40 U.S.C. § 3131</a> requires a performance bond and a payment bond before a contract of more than $100,000 is awarded for construction, alteration, or repair of a public building or public work.</p>
      <p>For a property broker, <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-III/subchapter-B/part-387/subpart-C/section-387.307">49 CFR § 387.307</a> requires a $75,000 surety bond or trust fund. The bond is filed on FMCSA Form BMC-84.</p>
      <p>State license bonds are set by the statute for that license. The face amount lives in that statute or on the insurance department or licensing board that enforces it.</p>

      <h2>What the premium is</h2>
      <p>The premium is an annual percentage of the face amount. The obligee sets the face. The surety sets the rate.</p>
      <p>On this site’s sketch, a $25,000 face at 3% is $750 a year. The principal pays the premium. The principal does not deposit the $25,000 face. The <a href="/#calculator">premium sketch</a> uses the bands published on the bond pages.</p>

      <h2>What happens on a claim</h2>
      <p>If the obligee has a valid claim, the surety pays up to the bond amount, then the principal reimburses the surety.</p>
      <p>That reimbursement right is the indemnity agreement signed with the bond. The surety investigates the claim before it pays.</p>

      <h2>What happens without the bond</h2>
      <p>If the license, the contract, or the court requires the bond, the license is not issued, the project does not start, or the court step does not proceed until the bond is on file.</p>

      <h2>How to get the bond</h2>
      <p>Name the form, run the sketch, and ask Integrity First Insurance to place it. Many license and permit bonds are issued in one to three business days. Larger contract files take longer because the surety reads financials.</p>
      <p>Company ratings and Treasury Circular 570 limits are on the <a href="/guides/best-surety-bond-companies/">best surety bond companies shortlist</a>.</p>
